    "ym-build-observable-core",
    "Define, test, and audit gauge-invariant observable classes for rigorous Yang--Mills construction and mass-gap arguments. Use when choosing Wilson loops, local composite fields, flowed observables, observable nets, or any class claimed to determine states, continuum limits, clustering, or the complete physical spectrum.",
    "---\nname: ym-build-observable-core\ndescription: Define, test, and audit gauge-invariant observable classes for rigorous Yang--Mills construction and mass-gap arguments. Use when choosing Wilson loops, local composite fields, flowed observables, observable nets, or any class claimed to determine states, continuum limits, clustering, or the complete physical spectrum.\n---\n\n## Abducer Integration Guards\n\n- Activate this skill only for `yang-mills-existence-and-mass-gap-target` or an explicitly marked Yang-Mills calibration task.\n- Treat imported representational-dynamics artifacts as restricted, finite, conditional, or support evidence unless a later proof discharges their frontier hypotheses.\n- Emit claim level, non-claims, hard gate, hypotheses, falsifier, and demotion trigger before any promotion.\n- Compose with the target-neutral proof-depth skills: pressure formalization, hard-gate selection, nontriviality audit, anti-definitionality audit, same-domain transfer certification, and declarative learning extraction.\n- State efficient representation requirements: preserved observables, morphisms or recovery maps, invariants, error budgets, equivalence policy, locality or cutoff budget, transfer interface, and falsifiers.\n- Do not let a transfer theorem pass if the target property is built into the bridge definition; require independently stated source and target objects plus a consequence delta.\n- Preserve the four-cycle, recursion-depth-one publication-consolidation limit for local subgoals.\n- Source provenance: `agentic/external/yang_mills_representational_dynamics/2026-07-20/skill_bundle/11_vscode_codex_skill_bundle/.agents/skills/ym-build-observable-core/SKILL.md`.\n\n## Mathematical Invariants\n\nPreserve the Yang--Mills mathematical object: compact simple gauge group, pure gauge content, gauge-invariant observable algebra, regulator/cutoff/volume/coupling regime, reflection positivity, locality, OS reconstruction route, continuum limit order, and Hamiltonian spectral target. Any representation, morphism, RG step, or restricted model must state which invariants are preserved, which error budgets are allowed, and which edge case or counterexample would break transfer.\n\n## Epistemic Discipline\n\nEvery output must state epistemic status, claim level, evidence citation, source artifacts, explicit unknowns, and validation handoff. Treat physical relevance, literature-gap warrant, and frontier comparison as hard gates; unsupported implications stay conditional, blocked, or `requires_prior_art`.\n\n## Failure/Demotion\n\nDemote or quarantine if the skill assumes a mass gap, clustering, confinement, continuum existence, regulator independence, positivity, locality, observable density, or theory identity instead of proving it. Disconfirming evidence, prior-art collision, target-equivalent bridge definitions, or missing counterexample tests block promotion to a major YM publication pathway.\n\n# Build the Yang--Mills Observable Core\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Specify the regulated configuration, gauge group action, spacetime localization, and state.\n2. Partition candidates into bounded local observables, extended observables, renormalized composites, and auxiliary gauge-dependent quantities.\n3. Define support, separation, involution, products, symmetry action, and continuum renormalization for the physical class.\n4. Test whether the class separates regulated states and is convergence determining.\n5. State the exact cyclicity or spectral-completeness condition required after OS reconstruction.\n6. Add observables capable of detecting topological, diffuse, and superselection sectors, or record why they are excluded.\n7. Construct a hidden-sector countermodel for every proposed incomplete class.\n8. Emit the smallest justified observable core and its unresolved density theorem.\n\n## Control points\n\n- Do not identify gauge potentials with observables.\n- Do not infer a full spectral gap from decay of one glueball operator or a finite set of loops.\n- Separate point-local composite renormalization from extended-loop renormalization.\n- Track observable norms and prefactors uniformly in cutoff and volume.\n- Require a dense generated subspace before upgrading correlation decay to spectral exclusion.\n\n## Output contract\n\nReturn: typed observable classes; localization rules; state-separation result; convergence criterion; spectral-completeness obligation; renormalization map; counterexamples; and epistemic status. Stop the spectral argument when low-energy sectors may remain invisible.\n\n## Packaged PRISM Output Contract\n\nReturn exactly the generated `BoundedDomainFrontier` JSON object. Do not paraphrase closed variant values.\n\n- Follow the exact generated JSON schema without extra fields.\n",
    "{\"agents/openai.yaml\":\"\"}",
)
